Marc Hartstein wrote:
 > kpd wrote:
[...]

 > What I feel you are misunderstanding is my
 > assertion.  I'm not disputing his calculation of
 > the number of "potions encountered by the PC, per
 > level, on average" (which I agree is what he
 > posited).

 > My dispute is that the distribution of potions of
 > (full |extra |)healing among those follows the
 > probability listed in the spoiler pot1-343.txt
 > under the PROB column.  The assumption that they
 > do so was key to his calculation, as he estimated
 > the number of potions of healing encountered per
 > level as PROB(healing) * potions/level * levels
 > visited.

Well, perhaps, but that doesn't quite matter. While
Derek Ray may be asserting that it is the abstract
number of total potions generated that is important
in deciding the truthfullness of the anecdotal
claims he is debunking, he's simply wrong.

What is important in evaluating the claim that games
frequently lack sufficient foo-healing potions to
make oneself a tank is the potions really available
to the PC in real play of the game of NetHack, and
those aren't "foo-healing potions in the abstract",
those are instead the ones that at least once pass
through the PC's hands.

Potions in shops the PC can't rob, potions in rooms
the PC can't find, potions in rooms from which the
PC is routed by flocks of monsters, potions in the
inventories of monsters the PC cannot defeat, or
cannot defeat without those potions going missing in
the process, or who flee to other levels and escape
the PC's further attentions, are not potions that
invalidate the claim under dispute: "In many games,
I didn't find enough foo-healing potions _available_
to make my PC a tank by alchemy".

Thus, similarly, the "potions of other probability"
aren't the nut of the discussion. It is the "potions
in hand at alchemy time" that is. So far as I can
tell, the ones guaranteed to be available are pretty
close to exactly the ones generated per the spoiler
probability.

Surely that is the conservative assumption when
deciding what is at issue here, which is _not_
"exactly how many foo-healing potions are generated
on average", but rather "whether an anecdotal 'I
often don't find sufficient foo-healing to do
alchemy to make my PC a 300 HP tank' could possibly
be a truthful claim".

 > My assertion is that a _significant fraction_ of
 > the total potions generated in the game are
 > generated through a routine which does not in any
 > way use PROB(potion_type).

I'm quite happy to grant you that claim.

 > I would estimate (based on my experience of the
 > game and my browse of the code) that this
 > discrepancy is far greater than the 20% you refer
 > to, but is probably somewhere in the range of
 > 100%-1000%.

But I'm not at all happy to grant you that claim,
because it ignores the "quite often different
accessibility" issues for those "foo-healing potions
of other probability".

 > I believe that an expert player estimating the
 > percentage of all potions generated

But, again, that's neither what I understand to
have been done in the first place, nor what I claim
here now to be a reasonable analysis of whether
"games with sparse or no instances of essential
resources" are validly reported, as several
reporters claim now and have claimed in the past, or
are the results only of lies, mental incapacities,
and incompetence, as Derek Ray bludgeoningly,
repeatedly, and insultingly claims.

 > which are potions of healing would come up with a
 > number higher than 5.7% (as I believe the true
 > number is higher), and I would not have disputed
 > an estimate using such a number (well, I might
 > still point it out if the number seemed off base,
 > because improving the quality of the assumptions
 > going into an estimate gives better estimates; but
 > in reality I probably wouldn't have observed an
 > error in the first place).

Well, one of the low quality assumptions going into
the estimates is that "potions generated" is the
correct metric, rather than "potions acquired", so
far as I can tell, so we may have to agree to
disagree.

